    {
      "type": "done",
      "answer": "Aligned, contiguous accesses generate fewer transactions because the coalescer groups addresses by cache-sector or transaction granularity before DRAM access, and the total transaction count depends on factors like alignment and address spacing [S4]. Specifically, when addresses are aligned and contiguous, they touch fewer memory segments, allowing them to be packed into fewer transactions [S1]. In contrast, strided accesses can touch many more segments, and misalignment can cause accesses to cross extra boundaries, both of which increase the number of required transactions [S1].\n\nSee page 47 [S4] and page 48 [S1].",
      "citations": [
        {
          "document_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402",
          "chunk_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402-p47-1",
          "page": 47,
          "text": "GPU Memory Hierarchy\n\nAccess Memory: One Warp Memory Instruction Generates Up to\n32 Addresses\n\n\n\n\n\n▪ The coalescer groups addresses by cache-sector or transaction granularity before\n DRAM access.\n▪ The transaction count depends on alignment, address spacing, access width, and\n GPU architecture.\n\nMemory Wall     CPU Memory    GPU Memory    SOTA Memory     Advanced AI Computing and Systems        47",
          "preview_url": "/api/documents/ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402/pages/47/preview",
          "source": "S4"
        },
        {
          "document_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402",
          "chunk_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402-p48-1",
          "page": 48,
          "text": "GPU Memory Hierarchy\n\nAccess Memory: Coalescing Packs Nearby Addresses into\nFewer Transactions\n\n\n\n\n\n▪ Coalescing is evaluated for one warp memory instruction. Aligned, contiguous\n  addresses touch fewer segments.\n▪ Misalignment can cross extra boundaries. Strided accesses can touch many more\n  segments.\n\nMemory Wall     CPU Memory    GPU Memory    SOTA Memory     Advanced AI Computing and Systems        48",
          "preview_url": "/api/documents/ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402/pages/48/preview",
          "source": "S1"
        }
